Both cache() and persist() are optimization techniques used to save an intermediate DataFrame so it doesn't have to be recomputed. 
The fundamental difference is control: cache() is just a lazy shortcut with hardcoded defaults, whereas persist() gives you the steering wheel to define exactly 
how and where that data is stored across the cluster.

Technical Breakdown :- Under the hood, calling df.cache() is literally just calling df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) for DataFrames.

The Default (cache()): Spark attempts to store the data in RAM. If the dataset is too large, the overflow automatically 'spills' to the physical disk of the worker nodes.

The Customization (persist()): With persist(), you can pass specific storage levels.
If you want to aggressively protect your disk, you can use MEMORY_ONLY.
If you want to compress the data to fit more into RAM, you can use MEMORY_ONLY_SER, which stores the data as serialized byte arrays, 
trading a little extra CPU time for a massive reduction in memory footprint.

Buisness Context :-
We use these commands whenever a pipeline 'branches'—meaning a single, expensive transformation (like a 30-minute join) generates a DataFrame
that is then used multiple times downstream, such as writing to a delta table and updating a reporting dashboard.
Caching ensures the cluster only pays that 30-minute compute tax once, drastically reducing cloud costs and pipeline duration.

Design Decision :-

There are two massive anti-patterns I hunt for in code reviews regarding caching.
First, juniors often forget to call df.unpersist() when they are done with the data, leaving cluster RAM bloated and suffocating downstream tasks.
Second, over-caching. Because disk I/O and serialization are expensive,
if your source data is sitting in a highly optimized columnar format like Parquet or Iceberg,
it is frequently much faster to just let Spark recompute the DAG from scratch rather than forcing a heavy persist() to disk.
